@petertraveller6421 LOL, so you don't want to touch the darn Scandies even  with a ten-metre pole? 
Hey, but you got a mountain as a gift from Norway. That's Scandinavia proper. 
But if you don't want it, we Dutch would be grateful for a few altitude metres :)

@OhirRipe There were opposition from neighbours to the railroad. People doesn't like making the line double tracked and increasing traffic, hence increased noise. My personal take based on living close to the railroad on the Danish side is a concern regarding freight cars with wheel bearings, which have a tendency to whine. However German opposition has now delayed the opening on the German side to after EU's deadline from having those bearings replaced or scrapped, so problem solved in that regard.

It's also a valid concern regarding closing all railroad crossings. Some, but not all are replaced with overpasses and if there is no room inside a town, an overpass might be made right outside, hence effectively cutting a town in two.

It's quite possible that there were non-Germans acting as or influencing German GNOs, but the concerns from neighbours is real, particularly those so close that they might lose part of the garden to make the line double tracked.
